3.4 Content-Level Expectations
Starter and Level 1 modules will emphasize foundational sentence production and short connected writing. Level 2 modules will extend grammatical choices and relationships between ideas. Levels 3–5 will progressively address complex sentences, precision, stance, and genre requirements.
Textbook CEFR associations will inform coverage without establishing individual proficiency.
The client’s aspiration for a 150-word composition will apply only where suitable for the learner group, purpose, and genre.
3.5 Alignment
The design will connect:
Course outcome → Age-pathway objective → Module assessment → Learning activities and feedback
Project goals will be evaluated through product and implementation evidence; learning objectives will be evaluated through learner performance.
Section 4. Content Organization, Module Structure, and Objectives
4.1 Organization Principles
The course will use ten content strands containing thirty proposed modules.
Modules will integrate content when it shares a communicative function and prerequisite foundation. Repeated textbook content will be consolidated into a common explanation, followed by progressively more demanding application.
Each module will include:
* A defined performance focus.
* Included and excluded content.
* Prerequisite checks.
* Short lessons and practice.
* An independent application task.
* Feedback and revision.
* Textbook references.
The thirty modules are curriculum containers. Their lesson counts and completion times will be established during detailed development and testing.
4.2 Full-Series Grammar Content and Module Map
The supplementary course will organize the supplied grammar content from THINK Starter through Level 5 into eleven strands and thirty-six modules.
Repeated content will be consolidated, while level-specific extensions will remain identifiable through separate lessons. Each module will contain a sequence of manageable lessons rather than present all listed structures at once.
Textbook Connections identifies where the included grammar appears in the supplied mapping. Coverage refers to the recorded grammar topics; it does not imply coverage of every textbook exercise or all listening, speaking, and vocabulary content.
