Coverage and Use of the Map
The map assigns a location to the grammar topics recorded in the supplied six-level summaries, including the previously omitted interaction, reference, request, and sentence-pattern content.
Welcome-unit connections follow the earlier overview. Topics appearing only in that overview, and Level 5’s detailed rule boundaries, will be checked against the source pages during development. Expressions listed only as “phrases for fluency” will be recorded individually when their lessons are prepared.
The same age pathway may include different textbook-level lessons. Neither pathway requires every learner to complete all thirty-six modules.
Module numbers identify content locations. Prerequisite relationships will determine recommended learning sequences, while documented learner needs and production capacity will determine development priorities.
4.3 Cross-Unit Integration
Integration will follow three procedures:
1. Consolidate repeated foundations.

 For example, Starter U5 and Level 1 U1 will contribute to a shared routine-expression sequence.

2. Preserve meaningful extensions.

 Level 1’s present simple/continuous contrast will remain a distinct module after separate preparation in both forms.

3. Separate similar forms with different meanings.

 Used to do, be used to doing, and get used to doing will be explicitly contrasted. Likewise, mustn’t and don’t have to will receive different contextual examples.

The course will not require students to complete every module simply because they are enrolled at a particular textbook level. Entry evidence and teacher guidance will inform selection.
4.4 Age-Differentiated Module Objectives
Age differentiation will inform task complexity, presentation, and instructional support. Access to particular content will depend on demonstrated prerequisites and writing readiness. Advanced modules will therefore not be compulsory for every learner in either pathway.
