	Given a supported event sequence, independently write sentences showing which event occurred earlier and which activity had continued.
	Given a narrative task, organize past events using appropriate past-simple, continuous, and perfect forms to make chronology clear.
	M17. Plans, Arrangements, and Predictions
	Given familiar future situations, express plans, arrangements, or predictions using the forms taught.
	Given a future-oriented writing task, select appropriate forms for intentions, arrangements, schedules, and predictions according to meaning.
	M18. Future Progress, Completion, and Past Perspective
	Given a clear timeline and familiar prompts, express a future action in progress or completed by a specified time using targeted forms.
	Given a future or retrospective task, distinguish future progress, future completion, and future viewed from the past using appropriate structures.
	M19. Ability and Achievement
	Given familiar ability prompts, express present or past ability using the targeted forms.
	Given an achievement account, distinguish general ability from success or inability on a specific occasion and select appropriate expressions.
	M20. Permission, Rules, and Requests
	Given familiar classroom or everyday situations, write appropriate rules, permission questions, and polite requests using targeted forms.
	Given a rules or interaction task, distinguish permission, prohibition, requests, and compulsion using appropriate structures.
	M21. Obligation, Advice, and Necessity
	Given familiar problems or responsibilities, express advice, obligation, or lack of necessity using the targeted forms.
	Given an advisory or reflective task, distinguish obligation, advice, prohibition, and unnecessary past actions using appropriate expressions.
	M22. Deduction, Evaluation, and Modal Precision
	Given explicit evidence in short situations, express certainty or possibility using the modal forms taught.
	Given evidence about present or past events, communicate deductions, possibilities, and evaluations using appropriate modal structures and degrees of certainty.
	M23. Checking Information and Agreement
	Given familiar statements, construct appropriate question tags or agreement responses using targeted auxiliaries.
	Given a short dialogue task, use question tags and agreement structures with appropriate polarity, auxiliary forms, and reference.
	M24. Real Conditions and Future Time
	Given familiar cause-and-consequence situations, write real conditional or future-time sentences using the targeted patterns.
	Given a practical planning task, express conditions and future consequences using appropriate clauses and conjunctions.
	M25. Hypothetical Situations, Wishes, and Preferences
	Given familiar imaginary situations, express a hypothetical choice, wish, or preference using the targeted pattern.
	Given a dilemma or preference task, distinguish hypothetical situations, present wishes, desired changes, and preferences using appropriate structures.
	M26. Past Alternatives and Mixed Conditions
	Given an explicit past-event timeline, express an imagined alternative and its result using the selected conditional pattern.
	Given a reflective task, express unreal past alternatives, regrets, and mixed-time consequences using appropriate conditional and wish structures.
	M27. Passive Descriptions and Processes
	Given a familiar process or event, write passive sentences using the tense forms taught.
	Given a process or event-description task, select active or passive structures according to the intended focus and use appropriate tense forms.
	M28. Services and Extended Passives
	Given familiar service situations, express an arranged service using the targeted causative structure.
	Given a service or event account, distinguish and apply causative, get-passive, and modal-passive structures according to meaning.
	M29. Reported Speech and Reporting Patterns
	Given short familiar statements or instructions, report their meaning using the selected reporting patterns.
	Given a reporting task, report statements, questions, requests, and instructions with appropriate word order, verb patterns, and reference changes.
	M30. Formal Reporting and Hedging
	Given short information statements, use a selected reporting or hedging pattern to distinguish a claim from a certain fact.
	Given a formal reporting task, use passive reporting structures and hedging expressions to represent claims and degrees of certainty accurately.
	M31. Verb Complements and Meaning Contrasts
	Given familiar sentence contexts, use the required -ing, infinitive, or object-plus-verb pattern after the verbs taught.
	Given contextualized writing tasks, select appropriate verb complements and distinguish meaning changes associated with alternative patterns.
	M32. Phrasal Verbs and Object Placement
	Given familiar phrasal verbs, construct sentences with appropriate noun or pronoun placement.
	Given a short writing task and permitted dictionary support, use targeted phrasal verbs with appropriate meaning, separability, and object placement.
	M33. Relative and Participle Clauses
	Given familiar people, things, or places, combine information using the relative-clause patterns taught.
	Given a descriptive task, use appropriate relative clauses and, in extension tasks, reduced or participle clauses to add information clearly.
	M34. Comparison, Modification, and Adverbials
	Given familiar descriptions or comparisons, use targeted adjectives, adverbs, and comparative forms to express the intended meaning.
	Given a descriptive or comparative task, use appropriate comparison patterns, modifiers, and adverbials to communicate degree, manner, and change.
	M35. Reference, Emphasis, and Stance
	Given a short familiar response, use selected reference expressions to avoid repetition and selected emphasis forms to highlight meaning.
	Given a text for production or revision, use appropriate substitution, ellipsis, emphasis, and stance expressions while maintaining clarity.
	M36. Purpose, Result, Contrast, and Cause
	Given related familiar ideas, connect them using targeted expressions of reason, purpose, or result.
	Given an explanatory or argumentative task, connect ideas using appropriate purpose, result, contrast, and cause expressions with correct clause or phrase structures.
