Section 5. Assessment and Feedback Plan
5.1 Entry Checks
Module-specific entry checks will examine prerequisite skills through short recognition and production tasks. Results will guide access to the selected module stage, preparatory support, or an alternative starting point.
The THINK Pre-Test will inform course placement. It will not establish writing readiness or replace module-specific evidence.
5.2 Assessment During Learning
Checks will include:
   * Contextual selection.
   * Sentence construction and transformation.
   * Error identification and correction.
   * Short written application.
   * Comparison of examples.
   * Revision following feedback.
Responses will inform whether learners need additional explanation, guided practice, or progression toward independent application.
Understanding can be demonstrated through examples and corrections without requiring technical terminology.
5.3 Independent Assessment
Independent tasks will use new prompts or information while retaining the language demands specified in the module objectives.
Permitted resources will be stated explicitly. Tasks assessing independent production will exclude teacher prompts, sentence frames, and model answers. Performance will be judged against the module-specific conditions and criteria described in Section 4.5.
Where feasible, comparable before-and-after tasks will examine changes in performance. A delayed task using a different context will examine retention and application.
5.4 Writing Assessment
A shared rubric framework will examine:
   1. Target grammar.
   2. Sentence completeness.
   3. Task coverage and relevance.
   4. Organization, where connected writing is required.
Module-specific descriptors will clarify expectations for each age pathway and content level.
5.5 Feedback and Revision
Self-checking materials will explain why an answer is appropriate. Open-ended writing will receive teacher judgment.
Teacher feedback will identify a manageable number of priorities and require a subsequent correction or revision. Review workload and response times will be agreed before implementation.
Section 6. Subskill Analysis and Lesson Structure
(update 4/29/2026)
6.1 Purpose and Alignment
This section identifies the skills learners need to achieve the module objectives in Section 4.4 and contribute to the shared course outcomes in Section 3.2.
Subskills are derived from the intended performance and assessment task, rather than from grammar headings alone. The analysis examines how learners interpret a task, make grammatical choices, construct a response, and review their work.
6.2 Module Subskills, Course Alignment, and Proposed Lessons
Within each pathway, numbers identify subskills for reference; they do not represent lesson or development order. Lesson titles indicate proposed instructional groupings. Their sequence and boundaries will be established through prerequisite analysis.
For modules spanning several textbook levels, the relevant content stage must be specified before detailed lesson development. The listed lessons represent the module’s overall scope rather than a compulsory sequence for every learner.
