	6.5 Progression Toward Independence
Support will decrease from worked examples to partially supported construction and independent application.
Learners who perform poorly after support is removed will receive targeted preparation and another opportunity to apply the skill.
Section 7. Delivery Platform and Learner Navigation
7.1 Planned Platform
Notion is the planned delivery platform. A technical prototype will verify access, navigation, response submission, and feedback procedures on learners’ usual devices.
7.2 Navigation Structure
The product will provide:
                                    * A starting guide.
                                    * Grades 2–3 and Grades 4–5 pathway pages.
                                    * Suggested module sequences.
                                    * A textbook-to-module index.
                                    * Grammar and vocabulary references.
                                    * Practice, assessment, and feedback areas.
Readiness information and teacher guidance will help students choose an appropriate starting point.
7.3 Participation
Participation is intended to be optional and self-paced. Modules will contain clear pause points and recommended next steps.
Account arrangements, access permissions, assistance needs, and submission procedures will be confirmed before learner testing.
Section 8. Prototype Design Specification
8.1 Prototype Scope
The first prototype will implement M01: Personal Information and States, drawing primarily on Starter Unit 1.
It will focus on affirmative be sentences and interpreting personal-information questions. Negative sentences and productive question formation will remain in M02.
8.2 Prototype Objectives
Grades 2–3
Given four familiar personal-information questions, learners will independently write four responses, with at least three meeting all of the following:
                                    * Complete sentence structure.
                                    * Appropriate affirmative form of be.
                                    * Relevant information.
Grades 4–5
Given a profile prompt with four required information points, learners will independently write a short introduction that:
                                    * Addresses all four points.
                                    * Contains at least three accurate, complete affirmative be sentences.
                                    * Presents information in a logical order.
Vocabulary for the prototype will be selected so that the required responses can reasonably use be. Preferences requiring other verb patterns will be excluded.
8.3 Learning Sequence
                                    1. Examine a short personal profile.
                                    2. Interpret the information requested by familiar questions.
                                    3. Identify the subject, be, and completing information in examples.
                                    4. Match forms of be to subjects.
                                    5. Construct responses using temporary frames.
                                    6. Correct missing or mismatched forms.
                                    7. Complete the independent pathway task.
                                    8. Receive feedback and revise.
                                    9. Apply the pattern to a different person or context.
8.4 Prototype Materials
Materials will include a model profile, explanation pages, guided activities, self-checking feedback, independent tasks, scoring guidance, and teacher instructions.
